From 1be1683251ebfcbe476c26e419d573dd10d047d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:35:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09/16] hw/timer: model the BCM2835 ARM timer, replacing the
 unimplemented stub

The block at ARMCTRL_TIMER0_1_OFFSET was an unimplemented device: reads
returned zero and writes were logged, so a guest saw a timer that never
counted.

The datasheet calls it "an SP804, but not fully compatible", and the
differences are why this is its own model rather than a reuse of
hw/timer/arm_timer.c.  The counter is 16-bit unless CONTROL bit 1
selects 23-bit -- not 32.  There is a pre-divider between the APB clock
and the counter, resetting to 0x7d so the counter runs at apb/126 until
something says otherwise.  And there is a second, free-running
up-counter with its own 8-bit prescaler, which an SP804 does not have
at all.

The countdown uses a ptimer and raises the ARM timer interrupt; the
free-running counter has nothing to schedule, so it is computed from
the virtual clock on read and latched when the control register
changes.  The APB clock is a property defaulting to 250MHz, the Pi's
core clock.

Protocol violations are reported: a load that does not fit the 16-bit
counter, a write to a read-only register, a read of the write-only
interrupt acknowledge, and a pre-divider and prescale combination that
leaves a zero rate.

Register layout, reset values and bit meanings are from the BCM2835 ARM
Peripherals datasheet, not from a guest driver.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BtE1mJ79fHUqzyPBaSgBKx
